[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) merged every report about dates as one issue and marked it known.
Read report by report, it holds three problems with three different fixes. Each one now has
its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: `parser` is fixed (`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu
locales`), and `hint` is won't fix, because the booking-v3 form is retired. Nothing in that
update touches the one-day move, so it stays open.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {filed} />

### `parser` — the shared parser reads dates month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The shared parser reads month first, so day and
month swap: 7 April goes out as 4 July. Dates with a day above 12 do not parse as a month,
which is why only some bookings move. This is the bug #4471 describes. Fixed: ticket closed.

### `hint` — booking-v3 tells people to type the month first

alder.physio, copper.studio and pale.harbour run booking-v3. Its date field prints
`MM/DD/YYYY` under it, so people type the month first, and the form reads the tenant's way,
day first. Same symptom, a different bug: fixing the parser does not fix it. Won't fix: the
booking-v3 form is retired, so the ticket is closed as not planned.

### `one-day-move` — the date shifts by one day, not by a month

These reports describe a date that moves by one day: Tuesday 12 May shown as Monday 11 May,
one meeting that landed on Tuesday in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto, an invite that arrived
before the booking was made. A month-first parser cannot turn 12 May into 11 May. This looks
like a time-zone conversion, and it needs its own investigation. Open. The desk had already noted
these reports (`desk-notes.json`); the cause table files them under `parser` by tenant, and
they stay counted there too.

## How the count was made

- One id, one report: the export holds some reports twice, so duplicates are removed first.
- A report's cause comes from its tenant and the way it came in (`causes.ts`), not from the
  intake tag. The intake tag is a guess from the reporter's browser and disagrees with the
  tenant often.
- The reports the desk read as a one-day move are counted on their own as well, and stay in
  `parser`, where the desk files them.

